\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Selected SMD performances for the subjective listening test. SMD composer statistics and their overlap with the MAESTRO training set are also reported.}}{44}{table.caption.47}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Quantitative results on the MAESTRO test set. Arrows \(\uparrow \) and \(\downarrow \) indicate whether higher or lower values are better. Best score is highlighted in bold.}}{46}{table.caption.48}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Quantitative results on the SMD dataset. Arrows \(\uparrow \) and \(\downarrow \) indicate whether higher or lower values are better. Best score is highlighted in bold.}}{46}{table.caption.49}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Ablation study on the MAESTRO test set. \(Attn.\) denotes component ablation by adjusting the attention window, or removing attention. \(Dur.\) represents the MIDI segment duration, $<m,w>$ indicates loss with masking and weighting. An asterisk * marks the best configuration used for quantitative evaluation.}}{48}{table.caption.51}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Metrics for comparing different PianoTeq configurations against the reference recordings. The best-matching configuration, used to calibrate all listening-test stimuli, is highlighted in bold.}}{49}{table.caption.55}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Listening-test results. MOS values with 95\% confidence intervals are reported. ``Most seen'' composers include \{Chopin, Bach, Beethoven\}; ``less seen'' composers include \{Rachmaninoff, Haydn, Mozart\}; and ``unseen'' composers include \{Bart\'ok, Ravel\}. Best score is highlighted in bold.}}{49}{table.caption.57}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of HPT and Score-HPT on the MAESTRO test set. Lower values are better for MAE and \(SD_{\mathrm {ae}}\), while higher values are better for Recall$_{10\%}$. Best score is highlighted in bold.}}{59}{table.caption.63}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Comparison on the SMD dataset. All models are trained only on the MAESTRO training split. Best score is highlighted in bold.}}{63}{table.caption.66}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Beat-level distribution of five-level dynamic labels in the MazurkaBL training set, Vienna4x22, and Batik-plays-Mozart corpora. All three corpora share the same five score-dynamics labels, but their label priors differ substantially.}}{75}{table.caption.70}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Performance comparison of the proposed model against all baselines on the MazurkaBL dataset. Per-task F1 scores (\%) are reported as mean $\pm $ standard deviations over 5-fold cross-validation. PELT is an algorithmic method with no trainable parameters, while ANN did not report this attribute. The single-task parameter count is reported per model instance. The best score is highlighted in bold, and the second-best is underlined.}}{78}{table.caption.74}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Ablation study of the multitask multiscale network with BSSL as input. Per-task F1 scores (\%, mean over 5 folds) and their arithmetic average are reported. The full model uses MMoE, temporal scaling $s=5$, 60-second audio segments, and data augmentation. The best score is highlighted in bold, and the second-best is underlined.}}{79}{table.caption.75}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Cross-dataset evaluation on Vienna4x22 and Batik-plays-Mozart. Models are trained on MazurkaBL and applied to both external corpora without fine-tuning. Per-task F1 scores (\%) report the best transferred MazurkaBL fold under the protocol described above. The best score is highlighted in bold, and the second-best is underlined.}}{80}{table.caption.76}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Velocity-recovery diagnostic for Diff-SFProxy. Init and Recovery report per-note velocity MAE in MIDI units $[0, 127]$ before and after optimisation; Gain $= (\text {Init} - \text {Recovery})/\text {Init} \times 100\%$. Within each instrument, the best gain is highlighted in bold. The $5$\,s configuration is the default used in the cross-instrument adaptation.}}{97}{table.caption.93}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Evaluation on piano datasets, where ground-truth velocities are available. Diff-Synth and Diff-SFProxy back-ends process $5$\,s segments by default. Best score is highlighted in bold, and the second-best is underlined. The bottom two rows are ablations on Diff-SFProxy.}}{99}{table.caption.94}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Evaluation on guitar datasets, where ground-truth velocities are not accessible. $\mathrm {MAE}_{\mathrm {Velo}}$ is therefore omitted. Diff-Synth and Diff-SFProxy back-ends process $5$\,s segments by default. Best score is highlighted in bold, and the second-best is underlined.}}{100}{table.caption.95}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces MAESTRO metric diagnostic. Real audio is compared with GT-note resynthesis using GT or flat velocity under the Salamander (default) or YDP Grand Piano. Summary rows show GT-Flat separation and SoundFont drift. Best score is highlighted in bold, and the second-best is underlined.}}{102}{table.caption.97}%
